    <h1>Mission</h1>

    <p class="mission-statement">Atomdrift's mission is to make supply-chain
    security accessible to the open-source community, regardless of
    platform.</p>

    <h2>Principles</h2>

    <ul class="principle-list">
      <li>
        <span class="principle-name">Local First</span>
        <div class="principle-desc">Our tools will never require a cloud
        provider or remote AI endpoint, though they may use one if you ask.
        <a href="/azoth/">azoth</a> runs on your CPU, and
        <a href="/scan/">scan</a> works with no account, no API key, and no
        network.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="principle-name">Zero Telemetry</span>
        <div class="principle-desc">Our tools never phone home. How you use
        them is your business, not ours.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="principle-name">Frugal</span>
        <div class="principle-desc">Analysis is expensive, so we do it once
        and remember the answer. Human attention is even more expensive.
        Every false positive is a bug &mdash;
        <a href="https://github.com/atomdrift-project/scan/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">report
        one<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a> and
        we will treat it as one.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="principle-name">Portable</span>
        <div class="principle-desc">Software runs everywhere, so security
        tools should too. We build and test on
        <a href="https://kernel.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Linux</a>,
        <a href="https://www.apple.com/macos/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">macOS</a>,
        <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/windows" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Windows</a>,
        <a href="https://www.freebsd.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FreeBSD</a>, and
        <a href="https://illumos.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">illumos</a>;
        and provide best-effort support for all
        <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/rustc/platform-support.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tier&nbsp;1
        and Tier&nbsp;2 Rust platforms<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="principle-name">Composable</span>
        <div class="principle-desc">Our tools produce output meant to be used
        by other tools &mdash; stable JSON, JSONL for streaming, and honest
        exit codes.
        <a href="https://github.com/atomdrift-project" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Integrations
        welcome<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="principle-name">Open</span>
        <div class="principle-desc">Our tools and rules are
        <a href="https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache&nbsp;2.0</a>
        and will stay that way. The
        <a href="https://github.com/atomdrift-project/traits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traits
        rule set</a> is public, and patches welcome.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="principle-name">Inspectable</span>
        <div class="principle-desc">Every finding shows its work &mdash; the
        evidence, the matched rule, and where it came from. Otherwise it's
        just security theater.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions, patches, or a platform we should be testing on? Open an
    issue on <a href="https://github.com/atomdrift-project" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>,
    or email Thomas Stromberg at <span id="mission-mail">t, at this domain</span>.
    Commercial support is covered on the <a href="/support/">support page</a>.</p>

